From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 14:49:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 27/29] fix(gastown): stop the witness reaper from outrunning
 its pre_start deadline (gcp-ntbf)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

polecat-worktree-reap.sh runs as the witness pre_start, which gascity bounds
by [session] setup_timeout (10s by default) and SIGKILLs on overrun. The
script issued one `gc bd show` per candidate worktree, serially. On winnow
that was 81 candidates x ~5.4s against an external Dolt — roughly 7 minutes of
work inside a 10s window, so every start attempt died in pre_start[0], and
after six failures in an hour the supervisor circuit breaker latched OPEN and
stopped respawning. The busiest rig in the city ran 26h with no witness at
all. It was not slow, it was arithmetically impossible, and the candidate set
only grows: the reaper is staged at dry-run and the native gascity reaper it
defers to is a documented macOS no-op (gc-zxxy), so nothing shrinks the work
set. gascity-packs was at 16 candidates on the same trajectory.

Two changes restore the invariant the script's own header states — housekeeping
must never block the witness from starting:

- Bead status for every candidate now comes from ONE bulk `gc bd show` with
  all ids, joined back to the worktree paths in a single jq pass. Cost is flat
  in the number of worktrees instead of linear. Results are keyed by the id
  the store echoed rather than the id requested, so the fuzzy matching in the
  bead lookup can no longer let one worktree inherit another bead's status —
  a no-exact-echo lands in the same "unreadable, skip" bucket as before.
- The run holds itself to a wall-clock budget (8s default, --budget /
  GC_REAP_BUDGET_SECONDS) well inside the caller's, and every external command
  is bounded by the time remaining in it. When the budget expires it records
  worktree_budget_exhausted and exits 0 having reaped what it could. A reaper
  that runs out of time must yield the start, not lose a race with SIGKILL;
  the deferred candidates are picked up next cycle, since the work is
  idempotent. run_bounded prefers coreutils timeout and falls back to a
  background-and-kill poll, because a pre_start that cannot bound its own
  children is the exact failure being fixed.

All four safety gates are untouched, as is the staged dry-run rollout. The
loop's field separator is the ASCII unit separator, not tab: tab is an IFS
whitespace character, so `read` silently collapses the empty leading fields an
unreadable bead produces and shifts the path into $STATUS.

Deliberately not moving the reaper off the start path into mol-witness-patrol:
{{.ConfigDir}} is the only stable handle on a content-hashed pack asset, which
is why pre_start was chosen, and a bounded pre_start no longer needs the move.

Validation: gastown/tests/test_polecat_worktree_reap.sh gains two tests, both
verified red against the pre-change script — the bulk-read test fails with
"bead status was read in 4 calls for 4 worktrees", and the budget test hangs
41s on a 2s budget before failing "a slow read is not bounded" — and green
after (6.3s). Whole gastown shell suite passes, shellcheck clean, go
vet/build/test and golangci-lint/validate_registry green.

Follow-ups: restoring winnow needs `gc session reset winnow/gastown.witness`
once this ships — the circuit breaker latches. Two pytest suites already fail
on integration and are unrelated to this change; filed as gcp-yrwk.
